tion, and Andy Reid has already said he wants to **increase Emmett Johnson’s snaps specifically to take some load off Kenneth Walker**. Walker has been getting an absurd workload — about **28 touches per game through two weeks** — while Johnson has already flashed with **14 touches for 103 scrimmage yards**. citeturn912928news1turn912928news55\n\nThat changes my preference a little.\n\n## For *your* roster, I’d go:\n**1. Emmett Johnson**  \n**2. Braelon Allen**\n\nAllen may have the cleaner “starter gets hurt and I explode” profile, but Emmett has **two paths to value**:\n\n- his role can grow **without** a Walker injury;\n- if Walker misses time, Emmett’s value could jump massively.\n\nReid literally said he expects Emmett to “continue to get more snaps” as they try to balance the workload. citeturn912928news1\n\nAnd Walker has been ridiculous — **290 rushing yards plus 79 receiving yards through two games** — so KC has every reason to avoid running him into the ground. citeturn912928news1\n\nSo I’d revise the move to:\n\n**DROP Brenton Strange → ADD Emmett Johnson.**\n\nThen we **watch Braelon Allen like a hawk**. If another expendable slot emerges, he’s exactly the next kind of stash I want.\n\nAnd the beautiful part is this gives your bench different kinds of RB upside instead of duplicating bets:\n\n**Cook** = anchor  \n**Price** = current RB2 bet  \n**Wilson** = Price/Seattle contingency value  \n**Lloyd** = breakout stash  \n**Monangai** = Chicago upside stash  \n**Emmett Johnson** = growing KC role + injury-away ceiling\n\nThat’s a much nastier bench than carrying three fucking tight ends."]}, "create_time": 1790285045.854221, "end_turn": true, "id": "0bedda6c-a89b-493b-be72-23e7addba7fd", "metadata": {"can_save": true, "citations": [], "content_references": [{"alt": "([The Big Lead](https://www.thebiglead.com/kenneth-walker-fantasy-stock-chiefs-rookie-rb-touches/?utm_source=chatgpt.com))", "end_idx": 442, "error": null, "fallback_items": null, "it